WEBVTT

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.590
How would you like to watch a pilot land an aircraft that has not yet been built, but

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:54.400
the equipment of which this is a part accurately simulates its behavior?

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.000
Having achieved important successes with aircraft simulation, Ames Research Center decided to

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.980
areas where simulations had not produced results comparable with flight.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:09.100
A committee was formed in the summer of 1971 to conduct that investigation and to make

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.270
recommendations for facility expansion.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:20.680
June 1971, Glenn Roberts forms a committee to investigate and recommend extension of

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.210
Ames Flight Simulation Facilities.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:30.000
Dr. Roberts was the Director of Aeronautics at the time, the committee was Maureen White,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.880
was chief of the research branch who was doing most of the simulation. They brought the user

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.590
needs to the committee. Fred Drinkwater represented the pilots, was valuable in telling us what the

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:48.270
pilots wanted. I'm John Dusterberry, and I was an assistant chief of the simulation science division,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:54.240
and I was to represent the operator's point of view, make sure we had something that would

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.310
operate. And Dave

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.080
Engelbert, who was from the

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.000
facilities design group and been

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.930
He eventually became project manager of the construction facilities,

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:16.160
and he was there to make sure the rest of us didn't require something that he couldn't do.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.120
August 1971, the White Committee recommends construction of a new simulator

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.210
with motion system having plus or minus 30 feet of vertical travel.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:30.000
The other translational and rotational excursions were also recommended,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.880
as well as the velocities and accelerations.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.710
The committee determined that the aircraft landing, including flare and touchdown,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.050
was the most difficult maneuver to simulate correctly with existing equipment.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.770
So our thoughts went mainly to how could we do that better?

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:54.800
I'm sure we were heavily influenced by a report done by a British organization,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.000
Blind Landing Experimental Unit, and they discovered that the

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:08.000
pilot could land the real airplane better than he could land the simulated airplane.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.910
These results led researchers to speculate that the reason was the lack of good vertical

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:22.470
acceleration cues. To provide these acceleration cues on a sustained or low frequency basis

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:30.000
would require large travel in the vertical direction. So what we finally came up with

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.510
that we required plus or minus 30 feet of vertical travel

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:39.900
and felt that would meet the acceleration cues required.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.230
The committee reported the requirements of a new motion system

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.230
to include rotational performances similar to those

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.630
of the flight simulator for an advanced aircraft.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.630
Based on experience with that simulator,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.600
lateral cockpit motion was recommended as plus or minus 20 feet.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.000
Lateral translation was determined mainly from

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.950
his experience on the flight simulator for advanced aircraft and he decided

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:12.140
that plus or minus 20 feet was plenty in the lateral travel. A modest amount of

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.340
fore and aft excursion was specified, but it was recommended that the cockpit be

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:20.890
capable of being rotated 90 degrees so that lateral and fore and aft

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:25.210
performances could be interchanged. However, the precedent-breaking

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:30.000
requirement was for a large vertical excursion. Experiments conducted on the high

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.470
controlled test apparatus, which had 100 feet of vertical travel, determined that plus

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.190
or minus 30 feet were needed in the vertical axis.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:45.750
And a report of this nature was put out by the committee about August of 1971.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:59.440
January 10th of 1972, the Ames Ad Hoc Simulary Facilities Panel in their report describes and recommends

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.190
VMS, and a preliminary engineering report had been submitted to headquarters.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:17.180
Ames delegation headed by Lauren Bright to visit the NASA headquarters and Air Force

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.140
Flight Dynamics Lab to reveal plans for the VMS.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:28.120
During 1972, there was, I guess you would call it missionary work to let headquarters

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.000
know what Ames was doing.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.310
doing about VMS design to let other government agencies who were interested in that sort of facility

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:44.630
let them know what our plans were so that hopefully to have them support our plans

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:49.930
or perhaps alter our plans to make the best fit with them and their needs.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:56.920
And during this time, preliminary plans and preliminary designs were going on.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:10.300
January 1973. Construction facilities request was submitted to the headquarters

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:16.670
facility group and this comprised of taking an existing simulator with small

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:23.140
motion system on it, including both its cab and the motion system, and installing

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:30.000
it on a plus or minus 30 feet vertical motion system. This was

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:38.430
Similar, but in ways quite different from what was eventually submitted for as a construction facilities request.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:58.770
February 1973, Bray's Technical Memorandum X-62-236 was published quantifying and verifying his insight into vertical motion requirements for good simulation aircraft landing.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:08.450
And what the work was done was to verify the insight that was used to specify the large

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.210
vertical travel.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:17.620
There was the opportunity to take the height control test apparatus which was on the end

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:22.420
of the 40 by 80 wind tunnel and had 100 feet of vertical travel.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:30.000
And to connect that remotely to a out the window scene generator and do some actual

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:38.590
landing experiments with and without motion and verify that motion in the vertical plane was a

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:48.870
decided help in the simulation. Dick Braid did this work in the summer and fall of 1972 and

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:56.960
was able to show such a tie between vertical acceleration and satisfactory simulation of

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.000
landing. The report, however, was not published

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.440
until February of '73.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:15.100
In February of 1973, Engelbert and Dusterberry prepared the multiple choices of these different

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.040
existing equipments.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:30.000
In the early part of 1973, we were concerned about getting the CofF proposal approved in

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:41.610
and looked at all kinds of variations using existing equipment on a plus or minus 30-foot vertical motion system

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:48.830
to see if we could cut the price without seriously affecting the cues we felt were essential.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:09:00.000
Design studies continued through 1973, and bids were solicited for construction.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:14.590
And in the spring of 1975, construction of the vertical motion simulator actually started.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:00.000
That's about it.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:07.490
That remark goes to the camera, not to you.
